cumbency": 0.0, "money": -0.38}, "rating_margin": -16.0, "sigma_race": 5.0, "sigma_total": 5.91, "baseline_margin": -12.52, "analyst_adjustment": -1.48, "key_factors": ["Kentucky's strong Republican lean (R+15 partisan baseline)", "Unanimous Safe/Solid R ratings from all ten expert raters", "Barr's large fundraising and outside-spending advantage over Booker", "Prediction markets price Booker's chances at only 3-5%"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point firmly to Republicans: Kentucky's R+15 lean, an open-seat environment still favoring the GOP, and Barr's roughly ten-to-one cash and outside-spending edge over Booker. The lone recent poll shows Barr up nine, and every expert rater and prediction market treats the seat as safely Republican, so a modest tightening from the consensus margin is the only adjustment warranted.", "watch": "A sustained Democratic-leaning national environment plus strong Booker debate performance could narrow the margin, though flipping the seat remains a long shot.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Mitch McConnell (R) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Mitch McConnell's retirement leaves an open seat in a state that leans Republican by about fifteen points. Andy Barr won the GOP primary and enters the general election with a major financial advantage, having raised nearly $10 million and benefited from millions in outside spending, while Democrat Charles Booker has raised about $1.1 million with minimal outside help. The only recent poll, from August, shows Barr leading by nine points.\n\nEvery major expert rater calls the race Solid or Safe Republican, and prediction markets give Booker roughly a three-to-five percent chance. The forecast expects Barr to win by a comfortable double-digit margin, consistent with Kentucky's partisan lean and the resource gap, with only modest room for a late shift if the national environment moves sharply toward Democrats.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0216, "margin": -12.8